  {
   "xml_index": 4,
   "xml_name": "PS7_004_Function All Positions Of An Item",
   "display_name": "All Positions Of An Item",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><code>L.index(x)</code> tells you where the <b>first</b> <code>x</code> is in a list. But sometimes you want <b>all</b> the places where <code>x</code> appears.</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">L =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(31,95,153);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;a&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;b&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(52,129,188);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;a&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;c&#x27;</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(74,163,223);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">&#x27;a&#x27;</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">4</td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">&#x27;a&#x27; is at indexes 0, 2 and 4</div>\n<p><b>Hint:</b> loop over the indexes with <code>for i in range(len(L)):</code> and look at <code>L[i]</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a function <code>positions(L, x)</code> that <b>returns</b> a list of all the indexes <code>i</code> where <code>L[i]</code> is equal to <code>x</code>, from smallest to largest. If <code>x</code> is not in <code>L</code>, return an empty list.</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b></p>\n<ul><li><code>positions([\"a\", \"b\", \"a\", \"c\", \"a\"], \"a\")</code> returns <code>[0, 2, 4]</code></li><li><code>positions([5, 6, 7], 6)</code> returns <code>[1]</code></li><li><code>positions([5, 6, 7], 9)</code> returns <code>[]</code></li></ul>",
   "answerpreload": "def positions(L, x):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(positions([\"a\", \"b\", \"a\", \"c\", \"a\"], \"a\"))\n",
