  {
   "xml_index": 5,
   "xml_name": "PS7_005_Function Split Into Groups",
   "display_name": "Split Into Groups",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A teacher wants to split a list of students into groups of <code>n</code>, in order. The last group may be smaller if the students do not divide evenly.</p>\n<p>For example, with <code>n = 3</code>:</p>\n<pre>[&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;, &quot;C&quot;, &quot;D&quot;, &quot;E&quot;, &quot;F&quot;, &quot;G&quot;]   -&gt;   [[&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;, &quot;C&quot;], [&quot;D&quot;, &quot;E&quot;, &quot;F&quot;], [&quot;G&quot;]]</pre>\n<p><b>Hint:</b> the slice <code>L[i:i + n]</code> is the group that starts at index <code>i</code> (a slice never goes out of range). The groups start at indexes <code>0, n, 2n, ...</code>, which is exactly <code>range(0, len(L), n)</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a function <code>groups(L, n)</code> that takes a list <code>L</code> and a positive integer <code>n</code>, and <b>returns</b> a list of the groups (each group is a list). An empty <code>L</code> gives an empty list of groups.</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b></p>\n<ul><li><code>groups([\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\", \"E\", \"F\", \"G\"], 3)</code> returns <code>[['A', 'B', 'C'], ['D', 'E', 'F'], ['G']]</code></li><li><code>groups([1, 2, 3, 4], 2)</code> returns <code>[[1, 2], [3, 4]]</code></li><li><code>groups([1, 2], 5)</code> returns <code>[[1, 2]]</code></li></ul>",
   "answerpreload": "def groups(L, n):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(groups([\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\", \"E\", \"F\", \"G\"], 3))\n",
