  {
   "xml_index": 6,
   "xml_name": "PS7_006_Function Insert In Sorted Order",
   "display_name": "Insert In Sorted Order",
   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>Remember:</b> a list can be <b>changed</b> (it is mutable): <code>L[i] = x</code>, <code>L.append(x)</code>, <code>L.remove(x)</code>, <code>L.sort()</code> and <code>del L[i]</code> all change the list itself, and everybody who refers to that list sees the change.</p>\n<p><code>L.insert(i, x)</code> puts <code>x</code> <b>at index</b> <code>i</code>, and moves the items from there one place to the right:</p>\n<pre>L = [10, 20, 40]\nL.insert(2, 30)\nprint(L)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[10, 20, 30, 40]</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a function <code>insert_sorted(L, x)</code>. The list <code>L</code> is already sorted from smallest to largest. The function must <b>change</b> <code>L</code> by putting <code>x</code> in the right place, so that <code>L</code> is still sorted. It returns nothing.</p>\n<p><b>Hint:</b> the right index is the position of the first item that is greater than <code>x</code>; if there is none, it is <code>len(L)</code> (the end).</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b> after <code>L = [10, 20, 40]</code> and <code>insert_sorted(L, 30)</code>, <code>L</code> is <code>[10, 20, 30, 40]</code>. After <code>L = [5, 8]</code> and <code>insert_sorted(L, 1)</code>, <code>L</code> is <code>[1, 5, 8]</code>.</p>",
   "answerpreload": "def insert_sorted(L, x):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# L = [10, 20, 40]\n# insert_sorted(L, 30)\n# print(L)\n",
