   "questiontext_html": "<p>The <b>average</b> of a list of numbers is their sum divided by how many there are: <code>sum(L) / len(L)</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Python facts you may use:</b> <code>len(L)</code>, <code>sum(L)</code>, <code>min(L)</code>, <code>max(L)</code>, <code>sorted(L)</code> (a new sorted list; <code>sorted(L, reverse=True)</code> sorts from largest to smallest), <code>L.count(x)</code>, <code>L.index(x)</code>, <code>L.append(x)</code>, slices.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a function <code>count_below_average(L)</code> that takes a non-empty list of integers <code>L</code> and <b>returns</b> how many items are <b>strictly less</b> than the average.</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b></p>\n<ul><li><code>count_below_average([2, 4, 6, 8])</code> returns <code>2</code> (the average is 5.0; 2 and 4 are below it)</li><li><code>count_below_average([5, 5, 5])</code> returns <code>0</code> (the average is 5.0; nothing is strictly below it)</li><li><code>count_below_average([9, 9, 9, 1])</code> returns <code>1</code></li></ul>",
