   "display_name": "Pair Up",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>For a lab, students sit in pairs, in the order of the list. If the number of students is odd, the last student sits alone.</p>\n<pre>[&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;, &quot;C&quot;, &quot;D&quot;, &quot;E&quot;]   -&gt;   [[&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;], [&quot;C&quot;, &quot;D&quot;], [&quot;E&quot;]]</pre>\n<p><b>Hint:</b> the slice <code>L[i:i + 2]</code> is the pair that starts at index <code>i</code> (a slice never goes out of range, so at the end it simply gives one item). The pairs start at indexes <code>0, 2, 4, ...</code>, which is exactly <code>range(0, len(L), 2)</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a function <code>pair_up(L)</code> that takes a list <code>L</code> and <b>returns</b> a list of the pairs (each pair is a list). An empty <code>L</code> gives an empty list.</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b></p>\n<ul><li><code>pair_up([\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\", \"E\"])</code> returns <code>[['A', 'B'], ['C', 'D'], ['E']]</code></li><li><code>pair_up([1, 2, 3, 4])</code> returns <code>[[1, 2], [3, 4]]</code></li><li><code>pair_up([9])</code> returns <code>[[9]]</code></li></ul>",
