   "questiontext_html": "<p><b>Remember:</b> a list can be <b>changed</b> (it is mutable): <code>L[i] = x</code>, <code>L.append(x)</code>, <code>L.remove(x)</code>, <code>L.sort()</code> and <code>del L[i]</code> all change the list itself, and everybody who refers to that list sees the change.</p>\n<p><code>L.remove(x)</code> removes the <b>first</b> item equal to <code>x</code>:</p>\n<pre>L = [5, 2, 9, 2]\nL.remove(2)\nprint(L)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>[5, 9, 2]</pre>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a function <code>remove_smallest(L)</code> that takes a non-empty list of integers <code>L</code> and <b>changes</b> it by removing its smallest item. If the smallest value appears more than once, remove only the <b>first</b> one. The function returns nothing.</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b> after <code>L = [5, 2, 9, 2]</code> and <code>remove_smallest(L)</code>, <code>L</code> is <code>[5, 9, 2]</code>. After <code>L = [7]</code> and <code>remove_smallest(L)</code>, <code>L</code> is <code>[]</code>.</p>",
