   "display_name": "Replace In A Tuple",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A tuple <b>cannot be changed</b>: <code>t[1] = 5</code> gives a <code>TypeError</code>. To get a tuple with one item replaced, we build a <b>new</b> tuple from slices and <code>+</code>. Note that a tuple with one item needs a comma: <code>(x,)</code></p>\n<pre>t = (10, 20, 30, 40)\nnew = t[:1] + (99,) + t[2:]\nprint(new)\nprint(t)</pre>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<pre>(10, 99, 30, 40)\n(10, 20, 30, 40)</pre>\n<p>The original <code>t</code> is unchanged: only the new tuple has <code>99</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a function <code>replace_at(t, i, x)</code> that takes a tuple <code>t</code>, a valid index <code>i</code> (0 or more) and a value <code>x</code>, and <b>returns</b> a new tuple that is the same as <code>t</code> except that the item at index <code>i</code> is <code>x</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b></p>\n<ul><li><code>replace_at((10, 20, 30, 40), 1, 99)</code> returns <code>(10, 99, 30, 40)</code></li><li><code>replace_at((\"a\",), 0, \"b\")</code> returns <code>('b',)</code></li></ul>",
